<BaseLayout title="How it works" description="O2AT proposes duplex optical Ethernet framing, capability negotiation, receiver credits and a separate audio conversion clock.">
  <div class="wrap page-intro"><p class="eyebrow">Architecture</p><h1>Packets travel. Conversion timing stays local.</h1><p class="lead">O2AT uses standard Ethernet framing inside a single bridged Layer 2 domain. The proposed receiver accepts data into an elastic buffer, then reads it using its own audio clock.</p></div>
  <section class="section"><div class="wrap"><ArchitectureDiagram /></div></section>
  <section class="section section-top"><div class="wrap split"><div><p class="eyebrow">The physical baseline</p><h2>A qualified 1G SFP link.</h2></div><div class="prose"><p>The mandatory baseline is 1000BASE-X at 1.25 GBd, providing a 1.000 Gb/s Ethernet MAC service after line coding. The v0.2 optical profile specifies 1000BASE-SX at 850 nm over duplex LC glass fibre. SFP+ / 10G is an optional expansion for aggregation and channel counts beyond an admitted 1G budget.</p><p>Optical fibre provides galvanic isolation across that link boundary. A separate coaxial reference, chassis bond or other connection may create a conductive path. Optical isolation must not be described as whole-system isolation without checking every connection.</p><p>O2AT is Layer 2. Ordinary IP routers do not route these frames. The proposal uses experimental EtherType <code>0x88B5</code> only for controlled evaluation; a commercial assignment and interoperability programme are still required.</p></div></div></section>
  <section class="section section-top"><div class="wrap split"><div><p class="eyebrow">A negotiated session</p><h2>Agree before audio starts.</h2></div><div class="prose"><p>Endpoints advertise only formats they can accept, buffer and deliver at their physical outputs. The source and receiver exchange capabilities, session parameters and cumulative receiver-credit limits. A mode that fits the link can still be rejected when a connected DAC, USB interface or clock plan cannot support it.</p><p>PCM uses big-endian, left-aligned 32-bit containers. Native DSD is packed chronologically, most-significant bit first in 32-bit groups. Conventional 44.1 kHz-derived DSD and the explicit 48 kHz-derived extension are separately identified.</p><p>Frames failing FCS or syntax checks are discarded and counted. There is no mandatory retransmission of late audio. Loss, invalid active-stream state or underrun invokes the stream’s specified local mute/restart behaviour; unrelated malformed traffic must not mute an admitted stream.</p></div></div></section>
  <section class="section section-top"><div class="wrap split"><div><p class="eyebrow">Admission</p><h2>Capacity is calculated per link.</h2></div><div class="prose"><p>For a packet containing <code>G</code> sample groups across <code>C</code> channels, the v0.2 payload is <code>P = 4CG</code> bytes. The packet grouping is limited by the 1,440-byte audio payload cap and a 125 µs packetisation target. Ethernet framing, padding, preamble and inter-frame gap are included in occupied wire rate.</p><p>Eight-channel PCM32 at 3.072 MHz occupies 815.9232 Mb/s with a VLAN tag on 1G. It passes the draft’s 85% direct-link policy threshold and fails an 80% threshold. Sixteen channels at that rate exceed 1G. This is a transport calculation, not an assertion that a DAC or host can deliver the format.</p><p><a href="/explore/">Use the bandwidth calculator →</a></p></div></div></section>
  <section class="section section-top"><div class="wrap split"><div><p class="eyebrow">Engineering diagrams</p><h2>Follow each workflow.</h2></div><div class="prose"><p>The workflow library contains editable diagrams for link setup, session negotiation, packet handling, clock control and proposed extensions. Each diagram carries its v0.2 or proposal status and a source link.</p><p><a href="/workflows/">Browse the O2AT workflow diagrams →</a></p></div></div></section>
</BaseLayout>
